#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157103 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "d6fce272-8b91-53f1-bfa4-32f098d8ee86" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-7836" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $x^2e^{x-1} - 2x e^{x-1} -15 e^{x-1} = 0$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}e^{x - 1} - 2xe^{x - 1} - 15e^{x - 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 2x - 15)e^{x - 1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0$. This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 15 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad e^{x-1}=0\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x^2 - 2x - 15 = 0$ can be factored as\n$(x+3)(x-5)=0$,\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,5$.\n- The equation $e^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because $e^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x$.\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-3,5$, and their product is\n\n$$\n(-3) \\cdot 5 = -15\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157104 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "f1a4b9b1-a6fd-504b-882f-df32959c35ee" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-7838" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $9x^2\\cdot 3^{x+1} = 16\\cdot 3^{x+1}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x^{2} \\cdot 3^{x + 1} &= 16 \\cdot 3^{x + 1} \\\\\n9x^{2} \\cdot 3^{x + 1} - 16 \\cdot 3^{x + 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(9x^{2} - 16)3^{x + 1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0$. This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n9x^2 - 16 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad 3^{x+1}=0\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $9x^2 - 16=0$ can be factored as\n$(3x+4)(3x-4) = 0$,\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3}$.\n- The equation $3^{x+1}=0$ has no solutions because $3^{x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x$.\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nand their product is\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{3} = -\\dfrac{16}{9}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd1a4993-dda4-5588-b641-a74428e632d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246545} {:answer/id #uuid "f20317ce-07fb-5614-80e2-3d13f049f478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246546} {:answer/id #uuid "961e6726-5191-5f1a-8d0d-1d671db69d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246547} {:answer/id #uuid "18daf0ca-4ee6-5d4b-b63c-65208884314c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246548} {:answer/id #uuid "f09f79e4-3e57-5fd4-84af-4611b82ef216" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd1a4993-dda4-5588-b641-a74428e632d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246545} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2ea8463-2c79-549a-9af5-ec171a72f770" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246544}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a64546d-9996-5249-99f1-1b63f1609794" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-100781" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x-3}-8x\\cdot {7}^{x-3}+12\\cdot {7}^{x-3}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x-3}-8x\\cdot {7}^{x-3}+12\\cdot {7}^{x-3} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-8x+12){7}^{x-3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-8x+12=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{x-3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-8x+12=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=2,6.$\n\n- The equation ${7}^{x-3}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{x-3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=2,6,$ and their product is $2\\cdot 6=12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b37b632-3406-521f-bedf-7e7deb54b0a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246552} {:answer/id #uuid "ee56b96d-cf9c-5ffb-aeec-1a84cbd031a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246553} {:answer/id #uuid "26dd3863-f500-5882-b2ec-702462991dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246554} {:answer/id #uuid "01b3dbbe-fc62-52ac-a886-a4271bb0f8d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246555} {:answer/id #uuid "a35562a8-74bb-5a5e-85a6-824cea317430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b37b632-3406-521f-bedf-7e7deb54b0a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246552} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec628ef1-df76-590c-bef8-9536d9150880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246551}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc28ec0a-4e61-5f28-bc0a-e54b135917a4" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-100785" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}{e}^{9x}-36{e}^{9x}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{9x}-36{e}^{9x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-36){e}^{9x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-36=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{9x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-36=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+6)(x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-6,6.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{9x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{9x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-6,6,$ and their product is $(-6)\\cdot 6=-36.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9e95178-6e18-5d98-8efd-f6786a592aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246559} {:answer/id #uuid "78b14b2b-787a-5568-a394-dbf37a8b3007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246560} {:answer/id #uuid "f59981e6-be96-5245-b249-cc717dfb8d64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246561} {:answer/id #uuid "acf61566-d03a-56c7-b1d4-c2f535e1926e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246562} {:answer/id #uuid "cc906f49-034b-5e7a-80f6-67ad1b8bfa88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9e95178-6e18-5d98-8efd-f6786a592aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246559} :answer-field/id #uuid "86058a2e-6e8a-5810-9dea-27bd7414930b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246558}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59fd1c88-e64b-571b-8fd9-be9c8ef64bf9" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-100797" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x}=13x\\cdot {8}^{x}+6\\cdot {8}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x} & =13x\\cdot {8}^{x}+6\\cdot {8}^{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x}-13x\\cdot {8}^{x}-6\\cdot {8}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (5{x}^{2}-13x-6)\\cdot {8}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-13x-6=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{8}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $5{x}^{2}-13x-6=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(5x+2)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{2}{5},3.$\n\n- The equation ${8}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${8}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{2}{5},3,$ and their product is $(-\\frac{2}{5})\\cdot 3=-\\frac{6}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d82d0161-aba5-53e8-8304-6aa5f3713602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246566} {:answer/id #uuid "041305fd-406c-5dea-95be-6318806a78da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246567} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f9150b-ab57-56fc-8ee0-7a5748d6c49f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246568} {:answer/id #uuid "10bea93c-00d5-5ffa-80aa-93c0e05a8362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246569} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdd8899-2a26-5acf-a55d-e85d442b403a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10bea93c-00d5-5ffa-80aa-93c0e05a8362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246569} :answer-field/id #uuid "16b4206f-eed2-5ac6-b11f-292370dc497b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246565}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11afc858-764d-5d9d-a321-3ddf5528f806" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-110801" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2-x}-{7}^{2-x}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2-x}-{7}^{2-x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-1)\\cdot {7}^{2-x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-1=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{2-x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-1=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-1,1.$\n\n- The equation ${7}^{2-x}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{2-x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-1,1,$ and their product is $-1\\cdot 1=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27e8451c-1243-5154-9e7a-055552f56dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246573} {:answer/id #uuid "faa24f8d-6337-545d-80eb-aea5aa8a3714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246574} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf6bf92-13ab-5e7f-9993-96e57e80945c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246575} {:answer/id #uuid "11582582-b868-5600-b934-1e283edb59fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246576} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a21f36-16e3-52e9-974b-2de73e107af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11582582-b868-5600-b934-1e283edb59fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246576} :answer-field/id #uuid "82d32a93-92a0-595f-bf19-f646be42095b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246572}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ddd6b32-1ad1-5a92-b6ca-5f243dfcc079" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-110803" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}{10}^{2-x}={10}^{2-x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{10}^{2-x} & ={10}^{2-x} \\\\ {x}^{2}{10}^{2-x}-{10}^{2-x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-1){10}^{2-x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-1=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{10}^{2-x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-1=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-1,1.$\n\n- The equation ${10}^{2-x}=0$ has no solutions because ${10}^{2-x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-1,1,$ and their product is $(-1)\\cdot 1=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd860533-6dec-57bf-b80a-f38ff42112c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd860533-6dec-57bf-b80a-f38ff42112c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246580} :answer-field/id #uuid "7920a49e-7f15-5858-bd91-d0dee0620c7f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246579} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70648b7f-95e7-53df-ab01-81cd523f0648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70648b7f-95e7-53df-ab01-81cd523f0648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246582} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ef73344-74ee-5489-8f5d-711fb3febee8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246581}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7ee32a4-8b6b-5018-a0b9-f3644820a1e6" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-264754" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2x}-50\\cdot {7}^{2x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2x}-50\\cdot {7}^{2x} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-50)\\cdot {7}^{2x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-50=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{2x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-50=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-25) & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)(x+5) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,5.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${7}^{2x}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{2x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eeb470f8-c23f-5e9f-b1ef-2340eab1cff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeb470f8-c23f-5e9f-b1ef-2340eab1cff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246585} :answer-field/id #uuid "85f876b2-0ede-5183-9fe5-01bc45cc87fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246584} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313d52fc-3afe-56b8-9ab9-104383e7c25e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "313d52fc-3afe-56b8-9ab9-104383e7c25e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246587} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab436e00-c814-5e79-8032-71eedf646119" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246586}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5558ef32-69af-5d52-86fc-4fc49c1c3c53" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-264787" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $4{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-4}=144\\cdot {5}^{x-4}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-4} & =144\\cdot {5}^{x-4} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-4}-144\\cdot {5}^{x-4} & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-144)\\cdot {5}^{x-4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-144=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x-4}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}-144=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ 4(x+6)(x-6) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-6,6.$\n\n- The equation $4=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x-4}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x-4}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-6\\,\\text{and}\\,x=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246588 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e7d94f3-1075-5869-95c1-9ce80ef36b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e7d94f3-1075-5869-95c1-9ce80ef36b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246590} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0f18529-5cd7-553b-aacd-e34689dff23f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246589} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cecc6f4-7165-5cb6-b454-7e2b47cd401e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cecc6f4-7165-5cb6-b454-7e2b47cd401e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246592} :answer-field/id #uuid "b86430fc-cfe9-597b-b302-7cfa4b7b9da6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246591}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13d3136f-c8ce-5240-a0b0-d1a910aed9ed" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-264791" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $3{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x}+6\\cdot {7}^{x}=-11x\\cdot {7}^{x}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x}+6\\cdot {7}^{x} & =-11x\\cdot {7}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x}+11x\\cdot {7}^{x}+6\\cdot {7}^{x}+ & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+11x+6)\\cdot {7}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+11x+6=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}+11x+6=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+2)(x+3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\n- The equation ${7}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "594e22ff-8b5e-589a-8daf-251b27267f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246595} {:answer/id #uuid "357ea630-a6e7-550e-add8-97f08f49b8ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246596} {:answer/id #uuid "7c26331d-2cee-58be-bca5-0ff24b7d828c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246597} {:answer/id #uuid "589966ba-6d09-5d2f-8fed-c2579386b944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246598} {:answer/id #uuid "cd36d26a-3ab8-572c-8754-4654c2a157ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd36d26a-3ab8-572c-8754-4654c2a157ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246599} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b2b601a-63c2-5500-84db-f5de16cae5c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246594}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3d7afbe-c052-55bc-910e-6663c48abfcd" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-88459" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+12x\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+10\\cdot {2}^{x-1}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+12x\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+10\\cdot {2}^{x-1} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+12x+10){2}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+12x+10=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{2}^{x-1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+12x+10=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n2(x+1)(x+5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,-1.$\n\n- The equation ${2}^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because ${2}^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-5,-1,$ and their product is $(-5)\\cdot (-1)=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53c402cf-39e1-59cc-92f9-e4939083a715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246602} {:answer/id #uuid "c30a685e-2771-5345-8bfc-27e401cd0703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246603} {:answer/id #uuid "ddcdab85-f46b-5348-aad7-99379677a0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246604} {:answer/id #uuid "6808e8ab-586b-52a9-9981-1999d75728be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246605} {:answer/id #uuid "2af8eaf1-3f7f-51fe-a959-646eb69f6fa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6808e8ab-586b-52a9-9981-1999d75728be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246605} :answer-field/id #uuid "62ee4f50-f24d-5033-ab7d-06034a781711" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246601}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9858ac9c-7073-5859-82e7-56fc2e1a0034" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-88473" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2}=7\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =7\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2}+4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-7\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+4x-7)\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+4x-7=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{6}^{x-2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}+4x-7=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+7)(x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{7}{3},1.$\n\n- The equation ${6}^{x-2}=0$ has no solutions because ${6}^{x-2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{7}{3},1,$ and their product is $(-\\frac{7}{3})\\cdot 1=-\\frac{7}{3}.$"}]}
